1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:06,266
2 December 1984. Bhopal was fast asleep.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,119
It was a winter night. No one had any idea
what was about to happen.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,314
In the north of the city stood Union
Carbide's pesticide plant.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:27,285
Tank number 610 held 42 tonnes of methyl
isocyanate. An extremely toxic gas.

5
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,527
Late at night, water got into the tank. The
pressure kept rising.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,621
And after midnight, this poisonous gas
escaped into the night air.

7
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:51,912
The gas was heavier than air. It flowed along
the ground and crept into sleeping homes.

8
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,571
That night, the night shift was on duty in
the plant's control room.

9
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:11,567
At around 11 pm, the pressure in tank 610
began to rise.

10
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,502
It is believed that while pipes were being
washed, water reached the tank.

11
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:25,651
The refrigeration system that kept the tank cool
had been shut down since June, to save money.

12
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,222
The flare tower that burned off gas was down
for repair. And the scrubber was on standby.

13
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,937
Every safety barrier had already failed.

14
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:52,323
Workers turned on the water curtain. But the
water could not reach the height of the stack.

15
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,971
People woke up to the sting of the gas.

16
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,762
Burning eyes, struggling to breathe. No one
understood what was happening.

17
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,994
People ran out of their homes just as they
were.

18
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:22,693
Thousands were on the streets. No one knew
where to run.

19
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:28,922
Many ran with the wind, straight along with
the gas cloud.

20
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,003
The gas was heavier than air, and it was
filling the low-lying areas.

21
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,639
Those who ran towards higher ground had a
better chance of surviving.

22
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,214
Carried by the wind, the poisonous cloud
spread over the whole old city.

23
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,858
And the city got no warning in time.

24
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,228
The railway station was caught by the gas
too.

25
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:10,169
Hundreds of passengers were on the platform.
Many collapsed where they stood.

26
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:20,770
An express train, full of passengers, was
standing at the platform.

27
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,310
If it had stayed, the people on board would
have been trapped in the gas too.

28
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,805
On duty that night was the deputy station
superintendent, Ghulam Dastagir.

29
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:42,746
The gas was making him sick as well. Still,
he did not leave his post.

30
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:49,820
Without waiting, he sent the train off ahead
of time.

31
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,973
And he warned the other stations that no
train should come to Bhopal.

32
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,966
By morning, thousands of people had gathered
outside Hamidia Hospital.

33
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:12,848
Some carried their children, some held up
their parents.

34
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:20,262
The hospital corridors filled with patients
lying on the floor.

35
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,871
Doctors and nurses had been working nonstop
all night.

36
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,032
The doctors did not even know which gas it
was, or how to treat it.

37
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,406
They kept rinsing burning eyes with water,
and did whatever they could.

38
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,294
In the first few days alone, tens of
thousands of people needed treatment.

39
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:55,925
But many people never even made it to the
hospital.

40
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,898
When morning came, there was a strange
silence in the lanes.

41
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:11,803
The belongings of those who had fled in the
night lay scattered where they fell.

42
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:21,912
Slippers, bundles, bicycles. As if
everything had suddenly stopped.

43
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,082
On the streets lay people who would never
get up again.

44
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,209
By then, the gas cloud had moved on.

45
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,148
In about two hours, nearly 40 tonnes of
poisonous gas had mixed into the air.

46
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,940
Thousands of animals died too. The leaves on
the trees turned yellow and fell.

47
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,815
In a single night, Bhopal had changed
forever.

48
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,077
For days afterwards, pyres kept burning at
the cremation grounds.

49
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:13,575
Firewood ran short. Often, several people
were cremated on a single pyre.

50
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:19,831
In the graveyards, graves were dug in rows.

51
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:25,918
Every faith, every neighbourhood had lost
its own.

52
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,266
According to the government, 3,787 people
died.

53
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:43,390
By other estimates, more than 8,000 people
died in the first few weeks alone.

54
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:49,485
More than 500,000 people were affected by
the gas.

55
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,770
And the deaths went on for years.

56
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,786
7 December. Union Carbide's chairman, Warren
Anderson, arrived in Bhopal.

57
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:11,533
He was arrested right at the airport.

58
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:20,375
He was held under house arrest at the
company's own guest house.

59
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,682
But within a few hours, he was released on
bail.

60
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,210
He was flown out of Bhopal on a state
government plane.

61
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:40,422
After that, he never returned to India.

62
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,051
The court declared him an absconder. But he
never appeared in court.

63
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,407
In 2014, he died in the United States.

64
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:06,682
14 February 1989. A settlement was reached
in the Supreme Court.

65
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:14,078
Union Carbide paid 470 million dollars. And
the case was considered closed.

66
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:20,836
The victims were never even asked. They took
to the streets.

67
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,722
Their question was: is a human life worth
only this much?

68
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,548
Most victims received just 25,000 rupees.

69
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,250
And even that, only after years of waiting.

70
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:51,249
7 June 2010. A Bhopal court delivered its
verdict.

71
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,955
Seven former officials got two years in
prison. And bail the same day.

72
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,704
After the disaster, the factory shut down.
But the poison stayed.

73
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:12,534
Hundreds of tonnes of toxic waste lay there
for years.

74
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:21,124
For years, chemical waste had been dumped
into ponds next to the factory.

75
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,047
With every rain, this poison slowly seeped
into the ground.

76
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,369
The hand pumps and wells in the nearby
settlements became poisoned.

77
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:42,296
People kept drinking the same water. Because
there was no other way.

78
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,913
Forty years later, in 2025, the waste was
finally removed.

79
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,354
But the poison under the ground is still
there today.

80
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:06,220
The poison did not stop with that night. It
reached the next generation.

81
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:13,085
In gas-affected families, many children were
born with illnesses.

82
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:21,188
Doctors recorded more cases of birth
defects.

83
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,288
Rehabilitation centres were opened for these
children.

84
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,785
Today, a new generation is growing up in the
same settlements.

85
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:41,972
Children on their way to school hear of that
night only in stories.

86
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,176
Children play in the shadow of the factory
wall.

87
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,615
On land whose poison has still not been
washed away.

88
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,602
Those who had lost everything did not give
up.

89
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:13,219
Women like Rashida Bee and Champa Devi
Shukla led this fight from the front.

90
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:20,951
Survivors walked about 800 kilometres, from
Bhopal to Delhi.

91
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,494
So that their voice would reach the nation's
capital.

92
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,451
Their demands were simple. Clean water,
medical care, and justice.

93
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:42,134
In 2004, these two women won the Goldman
Prize.

94
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,292
Every year, on 3 December, Bhopal lights
candles.

95
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,803
For those who never came back after that
night.

96
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,634
A statue stands in front of the factory. A
mother holding her child to her chest.

97
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:13,298
It was made in 1985 by the Dutch sculptor
Ruth Kupferschmidt.

98
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:20,930
On the walls around it, the victims' stories
are painted in colour.

99
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,136
These walls do not let the city forget.

100
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:36,353
A small museum keeps the belongings of those
who died that night.

101
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:42,926
Slippers, clothes, utensils. Every object, a
story.

102
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,981
Beneath the statue it says: No Hiroshima, No
Bhopal.

103
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,394
We want to live.

104
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,162
After Bhopal, India made new laws.

105
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:12,483
In 1986, the Environment Protection Act came
into force.

106
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:21,351
Around the world, safety rules for hazardous
factories became stricter.

107
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,863
Alarms, emergency plans, and people's right
to know.

108
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,861
Today's Bhopal is a city of lakes.

109
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:41,454
More than two million people live their
lives here.

110
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,880
But the world's worst industrial disaster is
a warning.

111
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,893
Human life can never be cheaper than profit.

112
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:05,167
Today, evening still settles over Bhopal's
lakes just as it always has.

113
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:13,290
The city has moved on. But some wounds never
heal.

114
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:19,925
The factory's skeleton still stands in the
dark.

115
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,914
Like a silent witness to that night.

116
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:36,806
Thousands of people died in a single night.
Hundreds of thousands still carry its burden.

117
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:41,784
For them, justice is still incomplete.

118
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,311
Remembering Bhopal is not just history.

119
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:55,005
It is a promise that this will never happen
again.
